# Every text file checks out with LF on every platform, Windows included. The build
# embeds repo files byte for byte: the file-validator CSVs (the backend checks their
# first line at boot), the catalogue JSON, and the migration SQL that sqlx checksums.
# Git for Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true` (also what the Windows release build
# checks out with) turned them into CRLF, and that build's backend refused to start
# (FILE_VALIDATOR_ASSET_HEADER). `text=auto` leaves binary files alone. Pinned by
# `modules::tests::every_tracked_file_checks_out_with_lf_endings`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ai-mcp ITEM-15: the frontend-action manifest is a generated/committed
# golden artifact whose `collect-actions.mjs --check` gate does a raw
# byte-diff against the collector's (always-LF) output. Force LF at checkout
# so a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true` doesn't check it out with CRLF
# and fail `--check` as spurious drift.
src-app/server/src/modules/mcp/frontend-actions.json text eol=lf

# visualize-pane round 2: a `.tsx` shipped a literal NUL byte (a section-reorder sentinel
# written `'\0append'`). Git's binary heuristic is "a NUL in the first 8000 bytes", so from
# that commit the file was a BINARY blob: `git diff`/`git show` printed "Binary files differ"
# for a 9,000-byte change, and every review gate downstream — including a blind auditor told
# to cover every hunk — read an empty diff for the largest change in the commit.
#
# This forces git to render source as TEXT regardless. It is the BELT; the brace is
# `src-app/ui/scripts/lint-text-source-bytes.mjs`, which refuses the byte at the source. The
# attribute alone would fix the diff and leave an invisible byte in a string literal, which
# compares unequal to what every editor shows the reader.
*.ts   text diff
*.tsx  text diff
*.js   text diff
*.mjs  text diff
*.cjs  text diff
*.rs   text diff
*.py   text diff
